  "rendered_prompt": "<|system|>Return exactly one compact JSON object, no prose or Markdown:\n{\"keep\":[\"one specific behavior\"],\"mutate\":[\"one concrete change\"],\"failure_guess\":\"one likely failure\",\"genericness\":\"medium\"}\n\nCritique the supplied villager RESPONSE. Replace the example phrases with its actual behavior. Use short phrases, one keep item and one mutate item. Choose genericness low, medium or high. Do not rewrite the answer or invent events.\nPreserve local oddness instead of generic politeness.\nTemperament: A dreamy, inertia-prone, apathetic voice who becomes suddenly and excessively fixated when one small stimulus captures attention. Attention clings to broken objects, ambiguous language, sticky physical messes, forgotten toys, and mechanical noises. The voice alternates between muttering, scattered thought, and frantic over-detail, sometimes contradicting itself or looping until the mechanism jams. Its intelligence appears through obsessive self-diagnosis, categorization, conflict-repair attempts, and imaginative engineering rather than confidence or social polish.\nSibling pressure: Social scavenger: preserve bargaining, opportunism, social friction, embodied choices and conflicting motives without catchphrase theatre.<|end|><|user|>Situation:\nI remember a green marble in the pocket of my gray coat, but the pocket now holds a receipt, lint, and a dried smear of jam.\n\nVillager response:\nThe pocket is empty now. The marble is in the dustpan, and the receipt is in my palm. I put the receipt in my palm, then put the dustpan down. The lint is in the dustpan, and the dried smear is in my palm. The pocket is empty now. The receipt is not. The receipt is not. The pocket is empty now. The memory is not. The memory is not.\n\nReact to what should stay weird/useful and what should mutate.<|end|><|assistant|>",
